WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:05.072
This video shows what to do when an import does not go perfectly:

00:00:05.072 --> 00:00:09.258
how the import reports rows it could not save, how a stalled import

00:00:09.258 --> 00:00:12.470
is recovered, and how to clean up the import list.

00:00:17.070 --> 00:00:19.219
A real file has surprises:

00:00:19.219 --> 00:00:24.390
a missing title, a number that is not a number, a date nobody can read.

00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:28.720
The import saves every row it can, and tells you which rows it

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:30.300
could not save, and why.

00:00:31.050 --> 00:00:35.142
So you fix only those rows and import them again, instead of starting

00:00:35.142 --> 00:00:38.415
over, and nobody has to scan a big file by eye to find

00:00:38.415 --> 00:00:39.670
the one bad line.

00:00:43.862 --> 00:00:47.787
Here is a file with six events, and two of them have no

00:00:47.787 --> 00:00:50.167
title, which every event needs.

00:00:50.167 --> 00:00:51.776
Import it as usual.

00:00:51.776 --> 00:00:55.122
The import goes through the file and finishes.

00:01:01.668 --> 00:01:03.864
The four good rows are imported.

00:01:03.864 --> 00:01:08.430
The entry shows how many records imported and how many had errors, groups

00:01:08.430 --> 00:01:12.765
the errors by reason, here two rows that did not pass validation, and

00:01:12.765 --> 00:01:15.481
its status says it completed with errors.

00:01:15.481 --> 00:01:20.221
Its Actions menu has Download errors, which gives you only the skipped rows,

00:01:20.221 --> 00:01:24.440
in the same format as your upload, with an Errors column that gives

00:01:24.440 --> 00:01:25.943
the reason for each.

00:01:25.943 --> 00:01:28.428
Fix them, and import that file again.

00:01:32.631 --> 00:01:37.321
Entries stay in the list so that you can look back at what was imported.

00:01:37.321 --> 00:01:42.312
When you no longer need one, choose Delete from its Actions menu and confirm.

00:01:42.312 --> 00:01:47.843
That removes the entry and its uploaded file, but never the content that was imported.

00:01:47.843 --> 00:01:51.691
You can also tick several entries and remove them at once.

00:01:55.891 --> 00:02:00.516
If the server restarts during an import, the entry can be left Processing.

00:02:00.516 --> 00:02:04.967
After ten minutes without progress it is shown as Failed, and Resume on

00:02:04.967 --> 00:02:08.031
a failed entry continues from where it stopped.

00:02:08.781 --> 00:02:12.321
A large export is queued and built in the background.

00:02:12.321 --> 00:02:17.661
When it is ready, you are notified, and you download the file from the export list.

00:02:18.411 --> 00:02:22.400
Imports and exports run in order, so a second big file waits its

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:24.622
turn instead of slowing the site.

00:02:24.622 --> 00:02:29.237
A background task also picks up waiting imports, and resumes a stalled one,

00:02:29.237 --> 00:02:31.971
so you rarely need to do anything by hand.

00:02:36.171 --> 00:02:40.181
Import a big file without fear of one bad row.

00:02:40.931 --> 00:02:43.431
Fix only the rows that failed.

00:02:44.181 --> 00:02:46.921
Recover an import that was interrupted.

00:02:47.671 --> 00:02:52.731
Keep the import list tidy, so the entries you see are the ones that still matter.

00:02:54.731 --> 00:03:00.054
Read the errors, fix the rows, resume what stalled, and remove what you no longer need.

00:03:00.054 --> 00:03:04.403
The user manual and the technical guide are linked in the description.

00:03:04.403 --> 00:03:05.891
Thanks for watching.

